 :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "224b1407-ecac-5a6e-8e6c-adc74ec895fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[3]{30}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555066} :answer-field/id #uuid "b209a21d-dff4-5944-8427-d75fa437d847" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91958ce6-c301-564b-89d6-6edfb21cb5a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-32616" :question/problem "![](images/26/26b1e5acba63b8ee11d35e12bf0c8fd6944b7a466c9eab258ef36f6429e8ec0a.png)\n\nWhat is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $320\\pi {\\text{ cm}}^{3}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=320\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 320\\pi  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 320\\pi \\cdot \\frac{3}{4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =240.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt[3]{240}=2\\sqrt[3]{30}\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c5c224a-0c47-548b-a66f-101b03f6a4e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius or Diameter of a Sphere Given Its Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17ce7ecd-d0ed-5840-93f3-0001b823057c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555045} {:answer/id #uuid "32707e5b-5823-5a1d-bd2b-ef75cdb2b376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555107} {:answer/id #uuid "760a156c-e362-5d1a-b5ea-f2896456664c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555267} {:answer/id #uuid "c9edd21a-02c0-5f4e-9d8c-e390fbf70430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555487} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4e4c09-aee9-5012-986e-68aab485dedf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9edd21a-02c0-5f4e-9d8c-e390fbf70430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555487} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a9e958-7605-5553-8817-a7378b83215d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8015d6e0-b5ed-541d-9586-128e40ee9b69" :question/math-academy-id "q-33799" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf8bd7c7679e1c1df6c336b48892964c7658ac877b5efc22b099b1e98129d9ac.png)\n\nWhat is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $\\frac{343}{6}\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=\\frac{343}{6}\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{343}{6}\\pi  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{343\\pi \\cdot 3}{6\\cdot 4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{343}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{3}}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{7}{2})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{3}}=\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$"}]}
